I see exactly what is happening. The issue is that your "Proceed to Action" button and your "Confirm & Submit" button are sharing the same physical space in the DialogActions container.
When you click "Proceed to Action," the UI switches from "Details Mode" to "Action Mode." Because the DialogActions container is **sticky** and positioned at the bottom, when you click that button, the browser is **retaining the hover/focus state** on that exact spot, even though the button underneath it has changed labels or states.
Here are the 3 ways to solve this, ranked from easiest to most robust.
### 1. The "Blur" Fix (Immediate Fix)
The easiest way to fix this is to manually remove focus from the button the moment it is clicked. This prevents the button from "staying highlighted" after the click is finished.
Update your handleProceed function in VoucherAuthorization.jsx like this:
```javascript
const handleProceed = (event) => {
  // Force the button to lose focus immediately
  if (event?.currentTarget) {
    event.currentTarget.blur();
  }
  
  setIsActionMode(true); 
  // ... rest of your logic
};

```
*Note: Make sure to pass (e) in your button onClick={() => handleProceed(e)}.*
### 2. The CSS :focus Reset (The "Professional" Fix)
Your buttons currently have a :focus or :hover style defined in your jaStyles or the standard MUI theme that is "sticking" because of the sticky container. You need to force the button to drop that highlight state when it is no longer the active action.
Add this to your jaStyles.proceedBtn definition (or inline style for that button):
```javascript
proceedBtn: {
  // ... existing styles
  "&:focus": {
    outline: "none",
    boxShadow: "none",
    backgroundColor: "inherit", // Matches your normal state
  },
  // Ensure the hover state doesn't persist
  "&:hover": {
     // Ensure this matches your standard button color
  }
}

```
### 3. The "Portal" Refresh (The Most Robust Fix)
The reason this is happening is that MUI's DialogActions uses a sticky position. When the content inside changes (switching between "Proceed" and "Back/Confirm"), the browser struggles to recalculate the hover state of the container.
You can force the browser to recalculate the focus by adding a key to your DialogActions container that changes when you enter isActionMode.
Change your DialogActions to this:
```jsx
<DialogActions 
  key={isActionMode ? "action-mode" : "view-mode"} // This forces a re-render
  sx={jaStyles.stickyBottomBar}
>
  {/* Your existing button logic */}
</DialogActions>

```
### Why this works:
By adding the key prop, you are telling React: "When I switch from Viewing to Acting, treat this DialogActions box as a brand new component."
When React destroys the old button and creates the new one, **it is impossible for the focus state to persist** because the DOM element that was focused no longer exists! This will instantly kill the "highlighted even when cursor isn't there" ghosting effect.
**I recommend using the key approach (#3) combined with event.currentTarget.blur() (#1).** That is the bulletproof way to handle UI transitions in MUI sticky containers.
